## B6's residual was UNDERSTATED. It was written up as "wave TIMING". Three faces, all constructed:

1. **AN ORDER FORK BETWEEN TWO HONEST NODES.** `traceOrderFork`: identical enrolled sub-lace, differing
   only in two OUTSIDER blocks. Same leader, same wave-end round under both maps — so not a stall and
   not a delay. Finalized orders `[10,20,30,11,21,12,22,31,32,13,23,33]` vs
   `[10,20,30,11,21,31,12,22,32,13,23,33]`: **the same twelve enrolled coordinates, permuted, and
   neither is a prefix of the other.**
2. **A TOTAL STALL.** `traceRelayStall`: a 3-block relay leaves the wave-END round occupied by the relay
   tip and NO committee block at all — zero enrolled ratifiers, `tauOrder = []`, every validator up and
   correct.
3. **A MANUFACTURED EQUIVOCATOR** — the same-creator/same-round pair the equivocation guard keys on.

## The fix

`computeRounds B participants` maxes only over participant-created predecessors, threaded through
`roundOf`/`maxRound`/`blocksAtRound`/`xsortBy`/`hasEquivInPast`/`approves`, `mkRoundCache`,
`fastRoundMap`, and `tauOrderFastImpl` — the `@[implemented_by]` FFI target.

⚑ **EDGES FILTERED, NOT NODES**, and that distinction is load-bearing: dropping outsider blocks from
the map entirely (what `compute_rounds_filtered` does for `tau_unified`) would have **SUBSUMED B6** —
`traceSybilOnly`'s wave-end round would have gone empty. Every pre-existing `#guard` is green at its
ORIGINAL value.

